Now, we can find the length of the base $AP$ of the pyramid:

\begin{align*}
  AP &= \frac{PC}{sin(30^{\circ})} \\
  &= \frac{3}{sin(30^{\circ})} \\
  &= \boxed{6 \sqrt{3}} \text{ feet}
\end{align*}

Next, we know that the base $AB$ and lateral edge $ABC$ of the hexagon have the same length. We can find the length of $AB$ by using $AP$ and the fact that angle $APB$ is $60^{\circ}$ (since it's the vertex angle of the hexagon and the alternate interior angle of a straight angle $APD$).

\begin{align*}
  AB &= AP * cos(60^{\circ}) \\
  &= 6\sqrt{3} * \frac{1}{2} \\
  &= \boxed{\frac{3\sqrt{3}}{2}} \text{ feet}
\end{align*}

Now, let's find the edge $ABC$:

\begin{align*}
