When a point (x, y) is projected onto the y-axis, the resulting y-coordinate is the distance from the origin vertically (ignoring the x-coordinate). So we're looking for a transformation matrix that pulls each point down vertically to the y-axis.

The matrix for translating by (-x, 0) (moving points left while ignoring the y-coordinate) followed by a scale of 1/sqrt(x^2 + y^2) (to keep the vertical distance unchanged) is such a matrix. We can write this as a 2x2 matrix M. Let's find M:

M = \begin{bmatrix} -\frac{1}{\sqrt{x^2+y^2}} & 0 \\ 0 & 1 \end{bmatrix}

A neat trick to finding a square root with radicals is to use even) powers and factors of 2:

