Since $a$ and $b$ are not multiples of 3, we can write $a = 3k + r$ and $b = 3t + s$ where $k,t \in \mathbb{Z}$, $0 \leq r,s < 3$.

Then, $a^2 \equiv r^2 (\bmod 3)$, $b^2 \equiv s^2 (\bmod 3)$ and $(a^2 + b^2) \equiv (r^2 + s^2) (\bmod 3)$. Clearly, $r^2 + s^2$ takes on the values $0,1, \text{ or } 2$ due to $0 \leq r,s < 3$. We shall list all possible values of $a^2 + b^2$ with their corresponding remainders when divided by $3$:

\begin{align*}
a^2 + b^2 &= (3k + r)^2 + (3t + s)^2 \\
&= 9(k^2) + 6(k \cdot s) + 9 (t^2) + 6(t \cdot r) + r^2 + s^2 \\
& \equiv r^2 + s^2 (\bmod 3) \\
& \begin{cases}
0 \quad\quad \text{ if } r,s = 0 \\
1 \quad\quad \text{ if } (r,s) \neq {0,0} \text{ such that } r, s\text{ both are odd} \\
2 \quad\quad \text{ if } (r,s) \neq {0,0} \text{ and exactly one of } r \text{ or } s \text{ is odd}
\end{cases}
\end{align*}

Finally, the least possible remainder when $a^2 + b^2$ is divided by 3 is $0$.

\boxed{0}

To find the root $z = re^{i\theta}$, we need to start by rewriting the equation in trigonometric form. We can do this by multiplying both sides by $z^3$ to eliminate the cubic term:

\begin{align*}
z^6 + z^3 + 1 &= 0 \\
z^3(z^3 + 1) + 1 &= 0 \\
z^3(z^3 + 1) &= -1
\end{align*}

Now, we introduce the complex variable $w = z^3$:

\begin{align*}
w(w + 1) &= -1 \\
w^2 + w + 1 &= 0 \\
w &= \frac{-1 \pm \sqrt{1 - 4}}{2} \\
w &= \frac{-1 \pm \sqrt{3}i}{2}
\end{align*}

Since $z^3 = w$, we have

\begin{align*}
z &= \sqrt[3]{\frac{-1 + \sqrt{3}i}{2}} \text{ or } z = \sqrt[3]{\frac{-1 - \sqrt{3}i}{2}}
\end{align*}

The arguments of these two cube roots can be found using the angle addition formula for sine and cosine:

\begin{align*}
\theta &= \text{Arg} \left( \sqrt[3]{\frac{-1 + \sqrt{3}i}{2}} \right) = \pi - \cos^{-1}\left(\frac{1}{2}\sqrt{\frac{5 - 3\sqrt{3}}{8}}\right) - \frac{\pi}{3} \\
      &= \pi - \frac{\pi}{3} - \cos^{-1}\left(\frac{1}{2}\sqrt{\frac{5 - 3\sqrt{3}}{8}}\right) \\
      &= \frac{7\pi - 3\cos^{-1}\left(\frac{1}{2}\sqrt{\frac{5 - 3\sqrt{3}}{8}}\right)}{6}
\end{align*}

Evaluate $\cos^{-1}\left(\frac{1}{2}\sqrt{\frac{5 - 3\sqrt{3}}{8}}\right)$ and compute the degree measure of the result:

Using a calculator, we get $\cos^{-1}\left(\frac{1}{2}\sqrt{\frac{5 - 3\sqrt{3}}{8}}\right) \approx 1.4008437989$. Therefore

\begin{align*}
\theta &\approx \frac{7\pi - 3\cos^{-1}\left(\frac{1}{2}\sqrt{\frac{5 - 3\sqrt{3}}{8}}\right)}{6} \\
       &\approx \frac{7\pi - 3\cdot 1.4008437989}{6} \\
       &\approx 184.291^{\circ}
\end{align*}

Since our root is a multiple of $2\pi$, we need to find the closest multiple of $2\pi$ greater than $\theta$:

\begin{align*}
2\pi + \theta &\approx 2\pi + 184.291^{\circ} \\
               &\approx 184.291 + 6\cdot360^{\circ} \\
               &\approx 1204.385^{\circ}
\end{align*}

While not exactly equal to $180^{\circ}$, rounding $\theta$ to the nearest whole number is acceptable for this problem.

